lost we have to  ensure that we are sticking to  guidelines that make us able to save  lives and that the virus not to be  spread so I would like Boris Johnson the  prime minister to self isolate for 14  days yes I would and okay well we'll be  putting that to you other people I'm  sure later in the show interesting and  to hear your take on that and now the  case numbers and the daffy numbers are  sadly still going up and are you worried  about how the NHS is going to cope in  weeks ahead as Boris Johnson says he  expects the situation to get worse  before it gets better one thing I am so  proud of being is a part of the NHS  family and about one hour ago I left the  most incredible team of people I know  that we have the resilience to cope with  this we are strong and we will do  whatever it takes to save every single  life we're going to do everything that  we can we can't predict entirely what is  going to happen going forward but what  we can do is save two people please  please stay at home if you can unless  it's absolutely necessary please observe  social distancing measures because we  need you to help us save your life and I  think it's always gonna be a rocky road  going forward I know that we can pull  together this is not about political  point-scoring it's about saying let's  pull together let's work together let's  roll out mass testing so that we know  what we're working with  it worked in South Korea it's done well  in Germany let's roll that mass testing  let let let us really understand what  we're working with and then if people  also observe the social distancing  measures we can save lives and Michael  Gove couldn't quite tell me exactly when  the testing is expected to be rolled out  for all NHS workers although he did say  that the government was ramping up in  the days ahead and how important is that  testing for people who are working in  the health service and do you think it's  been a bit slow coming I'm really  disappointed that we are not able to  test all NHS and care staff at the  moment these are the people who are at  the frontline these are people who need  to know whether or not they have the  virus or not so that if they feel better  if they're feeling poorly they can  return to work and keep working but also  they need to be able to keep their  family safe and their community safe and  I want to see mass testing rolled out as  soon as possible but it is absolutely  urgent that NHS and care staff are  tested and they have access to testing  immediately and I'm not sure it's  entirely fair that senior politicians  are having access to testing or in  frontline NHS staff who will go  into work night shifts day shifts you  know a double shifts at the moment can't  get the test that they need okay dr.  Rosanna Alencon thank you very much for  being thank you very much thank you
 

